<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lady of Cao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 450 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Señora de Cao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lady of Cao is the name given to a Moche woman of high rank whose richly furnished tomb was found in 2006 at Huaca Cao Viejo, part of the El Brujo complex on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Her tattooed mummy was buried around 450 CE with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ornaments and war clubs, suggesting that women could hold political and religious power in Moche society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao.jpg" alt="Lady of Cao" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstitution_de_la_Dame_de_Cao_(Mus%C3%A9e_du_quai_Branly_-_Jacques_Chirac,_Paris).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
